{
  "id": "proj-plywood-bookcase",
  "slug": "plywood-bookcase",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Plywood Bookcase",
  "summary": "A 6 ft bookcase from one sheet of plywood, with 29 in dadoed shelves short enough to stay flat under books.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 8,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Rip the 3/4 in sheet into four 11 7/8 in strips with the circular saw running along the clamped straightedge, good face down (a circular saw splinters the top face).",
    "Crosscut two strips to 72 in for the sides, and the other two into six 29 1/4 in shelves (top, bottom and four fixed shelves).",
    "Iron edge banding onto the front edge of every shelf and both sides, trim it flush, and sand the edges smooth.",
    "Clamp the two sides together, inside faces up and back edges touching, so one set of marks serves both. Mark a rabbet at the top, the bottom dado 3 in up, and four dadoes evenly spaced between them (openings of about 12 7/8 in).",
    "Rout every dado 3/8 in deep in two passes with the 23/32 in plywood bit, guided by the straightedge clamped across both sides at once so the dadoes line up. Test the fit in an offcut first: plywood is thinner than 3/4 in, which is why the bit is 23/32 in.",
    "Sand the inside faces to 180 grit now; it is hard to reach them once the case is together.",
    "Dry-fit the case, then glue the shelves into the dadoes, clamp across the sides, and pin each joint with brads from the outside. Measure both diagonals and adjust until they match.",
    "Glue and brad the 1/4 in back on every 6 in. A square-cut back pulls the case square and keeps it from racking.",
    "Fill the brad holes, sand, and brush on three coats of Polycrylic, scuffing with 220 grit between coats. Fix the top to a wall stud with the anti-tip kit: a 6 ft case can tip over."
  ],
